WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.539 --> 00:00:22.480
What do you think of when you think of Pirates?

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.869
Do you conjure up images of fearless sea dwellers,
cruising the high seas?

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:33.870
Perhaps you think of the more violent realities
– two ships, locked side by side, their

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.710
crews crossing swords, their captains dueling
to the death.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:42.530
Maybe you even imagine the ungroomed pirate
who’s been marooned on an island, biding

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:45.980
his time before he unleashes a bold escape
plan.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.820
How long before you think of Blackbeard?

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.219
Of Captain Kidd?

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:51.399
Black Bart Roberts?

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:52.800
Johnny Depp?

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:59.339
Those men were fearsome, exceptional pirates,
who used overlooked intelligence and feral

00:00:59.339 --> 00:01:04.299
charm to rob the Caribbean blind during the
height of all piracy.

00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:08.420
History has made them exemplars of the Golden
Age of Piracy.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:13.299
Beyond the top-rate pirates, somewhere on
down the line… that’s where you’ll find

00:01:13.299 --> 00:01:20.210
the English man born under the name John Rackham,
immortalized by his Pirate moniker, Calico

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:21.950
Jack.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:27.180
The Pirate Republic

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.780
The Golden Age of Piracy was well underway
when our protagonist Mr. Rackham first appeared

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.460
in another man’s spyglass.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:38.240
This was the “Classical” age of Pirates,
where Scallywags sailed the seven seas and

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.049
robbed the rich coffers of Spanish, French,
and English ships and colonies.

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:47.450
It lasted around 80 years, from roughly 1650
to 1730.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:53.050
Every great epoch needs a cultural center,
and for the Pirates, that was Nassau.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:58.020
Decades before loyalist settlers escaped the
American Revolution by fleeing to the Bahamas,

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:03.890
Nassau became an overnight pirate haven, with
rogues of all kind fleeing unfriendly ports

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:07.830
to congregate on one massive, pirate-friendly
island.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:12.549
Even though it only became a pirate port around
1715 – just a few short years before the

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:17.599
end of the Age – Nassau would represent
the Pirate way of life better than any other

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:19.900
ship, cove, or port.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.160
Pirates were a curious lot.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:25.799
They spent their days relieving hard-working
merchants of their wares, and they wasted

00:02:25.799 --> 00:02:28.760
their nights trying to drink all the rum in
the western hemisphere.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:34.299
You’d be forgiven for thinking that these
brigands and rogues were just Caribbean Vikings

00:02:34.299 --> 00:02:40.480
with bigger ships and curvy swords, but in
between all the violence and swashbuckling,

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.799
pirates could be surprisingly progressive.

00:02:42.799 --> 00:02:48.459
Many of the pirates that set anchor off Nassau
were once soldiers or merchants for one Crown

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:52.299
or another, and the system chewed them up
and spit them out.

00:02:52.299 --> 00:02:56.510
They were decommissioned, unprotected, or
otherwise abandoned by their government, so

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:01.099
they turned to a way of life that promised
independence and self-sufficiency, if nothing

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:02.099
else.

00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:06.810
Nassau and the crews that operated there became
something of a strange democracy, where men

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:11.680
who were committed to the pirate’s anti-establishment
code always had a place to stay and a voice

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:12.680
to share.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:17.559
In the early 18th century, the voices that
carried loudest often came from men whose

00:03:17.559 --> 00:03:20.900
names survived the ensuing centuries: Charles
Vane.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:21.900
Samuel Bellamy.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.069
Benjamin Hornigold.

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.249
Edward England.

00:03:24.249 --> 00:03:26.989
John Rachham [comedic pause] was not one of
these men.

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:32.810
He was a B-list pirate at best, working as
the quartermaster for Charles Vane’s crew.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:38.659
Rackham talked a big game, as many pirates
may have at the time, but the reality is that

00:03:38.659 --> 00:03:44.430
he begins our story as only a lieutenant on
a far more prominent pirate’s ship.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:48.199
Despite his lowly status, relative to the
other infamous pirates of his time, Rackham

00:03:48.199 --> 00:03:53.079
and his peers were enjoying the absolute heights
of the Golden Age of Piracy.

00:03:53.079 --> 00:03:58.239
Nassau was pure paradise – a centrally located
hideout that made it easy for Vane’s accomplished

00:03:58.239 --> 00:04:04.180
crew to escape out to sea, rob a few ships,
then high-tail it back to their Bohemian base.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:09.180
The island itself was heavily fortified, thanks
to both natural features and manmade defenses,

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.980
making it nearly impossible for individual
pirate hunters or navy ships to chase down

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.079
pirates that went to ground there.

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:21.250
Once on the island, a thriving pirate community
offered all the food, rum, and women a pirate

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:22.250
could ever ask for.

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:26.000
It was a never-ending party, thrown by pirates
and enjoyed by pirates.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:31.510
There were no laws, no rules, and opportunities
for plunder all around.

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:34.770
What could possibly go wrong?

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:39.340
Between a Rack and a Hard Place

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:44.610
For the first 15 years of the 18th century,
nearly all of Europe was enraptured in the

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:46.440
War of the Spanish Succession.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:51.370
Most of the great powers of Western and Central
Europe had some sort of claim to the Spanish

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:57.060
throne after the death of Charles II in 1700,
and the continent spent a decade and a half

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.020
sorting out the ensuing conflicts.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:03.410
By the time it was over, pirates had complete
command of the Caribbean, and were engaging

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:08.170
in all sorts of shameless piratey behavior
with impunity.

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:12.900
When King George the First realized something
had to be done to end the piracy, he issued

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:17.620
the Proclamation for Suppressing of Pirates
on September 5, 1717.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:22.350
All pirates had one year to turn themselves
in to a colonial governor and receive complete

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.960
clemency for all past actions.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:25.960
No tricks.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.250
No traps.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:31.760
Pirates could end their pirating and take
up a normal life with a normal trade.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:37.650
For an age often marked by near-absolute monarchical
power and, it was a pretty sweet deal for

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.250
a bunch of dirty commoners.

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:43.270
It may have even been a tacit admission from
the King that the government could have acted

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:48.840
better, or provided better, for those who
felt a pirate’s life was their only real

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.460
hope as a healthy life.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.840
The “King’s Pardon,” as it would come
to be known in Nassau, deeply divided the

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.120
Pirate Republic.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:00.140
Some felt that the English Crown prioritizing
the end of Piracy surely meant that the party

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:01.140
was over.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:06.140
A few may have even wanted to take those democratic
pirate principles and try to build a real

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:07.140
country.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:11.720
But the most vocal group were the “pure”
pirates, who declared they’d never bow to

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:12.720
the crown.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.140
Rackham and the rest of Vane’s crew were
squarely in the latter camp.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:22.210
So when George sent former privateer Woodes
Rogers to clean up Nassau and end the party

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:28.590
once and for all, Vane and Rackham felt obliged
to welcome the would-be governor with a pirate’s

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:29.590
greeting.

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:33.820
In July of 1718, Vane arranged for a small
group of ships to set anchor off the island

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:39.650
and fire at Rogers’ incoming frigate; he
also set a ship on fire and sailed it directly

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:44.820
at Rogers in the hope of destroying his retinue
in a slightly more theatrical way.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:49.440
Rogers ultimately survived the attack, while
Rackham and his crew escaped out to sea amid

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:50.440
the chaos.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:55.040
Vane and Rackham spent the next four months
terrorizing Nassau, Jamaica, and other nearby

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:56.040
islands.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:00.280
While Rogers slowly convinced pirates to accept
the King’s Pardon, Quartermaster John Rackham

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:03.340
was coordinating more attacks than ever.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:07.680
And so it was that protagonist Jack came to
his first real spot of trouble.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.680
In November of 1718 – four months after
Vane tried to blow up Rogers’s ship, and

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:17.020
two months after the deadline for the King’s
Pardon – John Rackham and his warmongering

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:20.710
friends set their sights on a magnificent
new prize.

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:26.510
This was a French warship, almost certainly
filled to the brim with rum, raw materials,

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:29.290
sugar, tobacco, and who knows what else.

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:32.850
Rackham and his friends badly wanted to take
it for themselves.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:36.880
Captain Vane gave the order to engage, but
he quickly realized the pirates were outgunned,

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:41.130
and their ship’s superior speed would not
be enough to best the warship.

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:45.500
Vane wanted to retreat and live to fight another
day – an idea that his bloodthirsty crew

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.500
was appalled by.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.230
It was a no-win situation for the veteran
captain: he could face immediate death by

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:55.470
fighting a hopeless battle against a massive
warship, or face a potential mutiny by upsetting

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:57.390
his increasingly violent crew.

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:02.330
Vane was in command, at least for the moment,
so he ordered an unpopular retreat.

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:07.130
But after the pirates had safely escaped the
French ship, the crew of 70 were predictably

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:08.780
mad at the lack of a score.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:12.840
Ever the democracy, Vane’s pirates took
a vote, and the vast majority of them were

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:14.610
ready for a change in leadership.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:18.930
Charles Vane and his band of supporters were
given a small schooner they had captured days

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:20.740
before, and they weresent away.

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:25.060
In his place, the crew had elected a new man,
one who appeared more capable of delivering

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:59.940
the mayhem and bloody piracy the crew was
searching for: Calico Jack Rackham.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:15.520
A Memorable Wardrobe; A Forgettable Pirate

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:54.990
Many 
a pirate that

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:59.579
sailed through the Caribbean developed some
sort of nickname, and newly minted pirate

00:09:59.579 --> 00:10:01.970
captain John Rackham was no exception.

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:06.519
Rackham had a habit of wearing eye-catching
clothing made from colorful Indian Calico

00:10:06.519 --> 00:10:10.389
cloth; thus, Calico Jack was born on the high
seas.

00:10:10.389 --> 00:10:15.110
Unfortunately, it didn’t take long for Rackham
to realize he made a much better quartermaster

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:16.110
than he did a captain.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:20.949
It was easy to advocate for bold plans and
violent delights as a crew member, but Calico

00:10:20.949 --> 00:10:26.350
Jack soon found that designing and executing
glorious pirate plans was a lot harder than

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:27.350
it looked.

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:31.350
Like a modern-day politician who regrets the
promises he made on the way to his election,

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:36.509
Calico Jack suddenly had to provide all the
plundering that his peers had demanded from

00:10:36.509 --> 00:10:37.509
Vane.

00:10:37.509 --> 00:10:42.230
What’s more, he had to do it while Governor
Woodes Rogers had begun executing pirates

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:43.850
and cleaning up the region.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:48.089
Rackham started small, targeting low-rent
merchants or undefended coastline.

00:10:48.089 --> 00:10:53.170
He found modest success, and even executed
a crafty plan that made it into the pages

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:56.620
of Captain Charles Johnson’s A General History
of Pyrates.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:11:01.110
According to Johnson, Calico Jack and crew
had parked their sloop off the coast of Cuba,

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:05.949
and a few men had gone ashore to resupply
the ship with fresh food, rum, and ammunition.

00:11:05.949 --> 00:11:09.970
At low tide, as the sun was setting, a Spanish
gunship responsible for patrolling the Cuban

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:13.430
coastline had sailed into view and spotted
the pirates.

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:17.100
This was a powerful ship, that already had
a captured English sloop in tow.

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:21.449
The Spanish couldn’t mount a proper attack
at low tide, so they decided to wait until

00:11:21.449 --> 00:11:24.089
morning, then attack the pirates at first
light.

00:11:24.089 --> 00:11:27.860
Calico Jack and his Crew spotted the Spanish
warship and devised a plan.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:32.079
Rather than try to escape from the port at
low tide, Rackham ordered the ship abandoned,

00:11:32.079 --> 00:11:33.970
as everyone fled to shore.

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:38.499
As the Spanish ship fired on the pirate sloop
at daybreak, the pirates snuck aboard the

00:11:38.499 --> 00:11:43.000
captured English sloop, incapacitated the
guards, and sailed away before the frigate

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:44.610
could realize what had happened.

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:48.279
It was a nifty move, and one that likely marked
the high point of Rackham’s tenure as a

00:11:48.279 --> 00:11:49.319
pirate captain.

00:11:49.319 --> 00:11:53.550
But Captain Jack had just as many setbacks
as he did successes.

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:58.819
In December of 1718, for example, he targeted
a large merchant ship called the Kingston

00:11:58.819 --> 00:12:03.550
for, ahem, repossession, but drew up a lousy
plan that almost got he and his crew killed.

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:08.089
Rackham liked the Kingston because it was
carrying a hugely valuable cargo, but didn’t

00:12:08.089 --> 00:12:13.779
wait to seize the ship at a tactically isolated
area; instead, the crew jumped aboard within

00:12:13.779 --> 00:12:18.509
sight of Jamaica’s Port Royal, where the
pirates could easily be identified and tracked

00:12:18.509 --> 00:12:19.899
by the ship’s merchants.

00:12:19.899 --> 00:12:24.629
They hired bounty hunters, who caught up with
the ship several weeks later, at Isla de los

00:12:24.629 --> 00:12:26.920
Pinos, off the western end of Cuba.

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:31.861
Historians are unsure what happened next – some
report that Calico Jack and his crew had all

00:12:31.861 --> 00:12:36.980
gone to land and passed out drunk, leaving
their conquest undefended with the loot still

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:37.980
aboard.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.079
Other versions suggest that Jack was a coward,
opting to hide in the woods rather than fight

00:12:42.079 --> 00:12:43.079
the hunters.

00:12:43.079 --> 00:12:47.129
Either way, Rackham was in no position to
do anything about it, and he quickly lost

00:12:47.129 --> 00:12:51.060
his best capture before he had the chance
to make any money off the score.

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:56.350
Calico Jack wasn’t disciplined enough to
consistently pull off successful pirate heists,

00:12:56.350 --> 00:13:01.290
and he wasn’t foolhardy enough to launch
blind attacks and let the doubloons fall where

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:02.290
they may.

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:07.041
So a few months after overthrowing his captain
and living large at sea, Rackham and his crew

00:13:07.041 --> 00:13:11.959
decided to give up, return to Nassau, and
try to convince Governor Rogers to extend

00:13:11.959 --> 00:13:14.769
them a pardon, months after the King’s deadline.

00:13:14.769 --> 00:13:18.750
Upon landing at Nassau, Rackham and his crew
were bound and taken before Rogers, where

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:23.649
Calico Jack informed him that deposed captain
Charles Vane was the real enemy here.

00:13:23.649 --> 00:13:26.100
You see, none of them actually wanted to be
pirates!

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:31.490
But Vane had pressed them into service, forcing
them to do rob, rape, and ransack every village

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:32.879
in the West Indies.

00:13:32.879 --> 00:13:38.569
Once wholesome John Rackham had let a successful
mutiny against Vane, those good-hearted pirates

00:13:38.569 --> 00:13:42.860
sailed straight back to Nassau, hoping to
return to normal, productive lives under the

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:43.970
English Crown.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:46.899
It was, quite accidentally, a completely brilliant
plan.

00:13:46.899 --> 00:13:50.839
Governor Rogers had never forgiven Vane for
the fireship stunt upon his arrival and had

00:13:50.839 --> 00:13:52.730
continued to hold a grudge against him.

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:57.850
So in 1719, Rogers officially pardoned Calico
Jack and his crew, and they all settled down

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:02.389
to live a clean, decent life, free of piracy.

00:14:02.389 --> 00:14:08.069
Annie, Get Your Flintlock Pistol

00:14:08.069 --> 00:14:13.269
For all we know, John Rackham genuinely did
mean to give up his life on the lam when he

00:14:13.269 --> 00:14:16.089
returned to Nassau and accepted Woodes Roger’s
pardon.

00:14:16.089 --> 00:14:21.629
However, Rackham hadn’t accounted for the
one variable that can make a man do just about

00:14:21.629 --> 00:14:22.629
anything.

00:14:22.629 --> 00:14:27.550
It wasn’t long back on land before our former
pirate captain ran into a ravishing, red-haired

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:29.739
woman in a Bohemian pub.

00:14:29.739 --> 00:14:33.889
Anne Bonny was an Irish lass that immediately
captured Rackham’s attention.

00:14:33.889 --> 00:14:37.629
The two had instant chemistry and were immediately
smitten with each other.

00:14:37.629 --> 00:14:42.709
As Rackham settled back into civilian life,
he wanted to take Anne as his wife, to have

00:14:42.709 --> 00:14:47.579
and to hold, as they lived out their days
in the sun and the sand.

00:14:47.579 --> 00:14:49.639
There was just one problem.

00:14:49.639 --> 00:14:50.639
Anne was already married.

00:14:50.639 --> 00:14:55.269
Anne must have had a type, because her husband
was also a former pirate named John.

00:14:55.269 --> 00:14:59.939
John Bonny had happily taken the pardon and
accepted a new job, working directly for Governor

00:14:59.939 --> 00:15:03.160
Rogers as an informant on pirate activity.

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:08.450
In short, he was a snitch, helping Rogers
bring the remaining Pirates of the Caribbean

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:09.450
to justice.

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:12.740
Rackham and Anne Bonny tried everything they
could think of in order to start a new life

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:13.740
together.

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:17.420
They pleaded directly to John Bonny, with
Anne declaring her love for another man.

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:21.980
When he said no, they took their case to Rogers,
asking that he consider annulling the marriage

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:25.490
as provincial governor and John Bonny’s
boss.

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:31.589
In a proposal that’s both sweet and sexist,
Rackham even suggested “Divorce Via Purchase”

00:15:31.589 --> 00:15:35.279
as an option, offering to buy Anne from John
Bonny.

00:15:35.279 --> 00:15:36.279
Nothing worked.

00:15:36.279 --> 00:15:41.089
With no other options available to them, Calico
Jack and his lover Anne Bonny decided a Pirate’s

00:15:41.089 --> 00:15:43.160
Life was their last recourse.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:48.679
So in August of 1720, Jack, Anne, and a few
other friendly ex-pirates stole a ship from

00:15:48.679 --> 00:15:51.319
Nassau Harbor and returned to the sea.

00:15:51.319 --> 00:15:55.170
Anne Bonny may very well be the reason we
remember Calico Jack at all.

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:59.480
Many sailors at the time believed that having
a woman aboard a ship was bad luck – even

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:04.149
well-respected pirates like Blackbeard wouldn’t
allow them on his ship, let alone take up

00:16:04.149 --> 00:16:06.079
arms alongside one.

00:16:06.079 --> 00:16:11.199
Successful female pirates were awfully rare,
even during the Golden Age of Piracy, which

00:16:11.199 --> 00:16:16.769
explains why some sources report that Anne
may have hidden her gender from everyone but

00:16:16.769 --> 00:16:19.239
her lover and co-captain.

00:16:19.239 --> 00:16:23.629
During Calico Jack’s second tour of pirate
duty, some records seem to indicate that it

00:16:23.629 --> 00:16:27.360
was Anne who was both the brains and the brawn
of the operation.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:31.610
Years of living through a bad marriage had
given her an edge, which she unleashed by

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:37.610
seizing dozens of ships and killing any captives
who might threaten to undermine the operation

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:39.720
or rat them out to provincial governor.

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:44.920
There’s plenty of legends that have raised
up around Anne Bonny, the Pirate Lass, including

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:50.839
the time she tricked a French crew into surrendering
by standing over a bloodied mannequin with

00:16:50.839 --> 00:16:51.839
a raised axe.

00:16:51.839 --> 00:16:56.829
Rackham, by contrast, was mostly enjoying
the sex and partying that a piracy lifestyle

00:16:56.829 --> 00:16:57.829
afforded him.

00:16:57.829 --> 00:17:01.630
He enjoyed his new life with Anne, unencumbered
by the burdens of proper civilization, and

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:05.500
would often invite sailors from captured ships
to drink with him.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:07.589
Some even stayed on as new crew members.

00:17:07.589 --> 00:17:12.449
One such sailor who joined Calico Jack’s
entourage was yet another woman in disguise:

00:17:12.449 --> 00:17:13.449
Mary Read.

00:17:13.449 --> 00:17:17.559
Anne and Mary became fast friends, with each
seeing through the other’s disguise.

00:17:17.559 --> 00:17:22.250
Both were elated to find some female companionship
on the open ocean and began confiding in each

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:23.250
other.

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:26.760
In a bit that might as well be pulled straight
from 12th Night, Calico Jack began to grow

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:31.590
jealous of the gabby lad who Anne was suddenly
spending so much time with.

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:34.040
Eventually, Anne had to cue her man in on
Mary’s secret!

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:38.110
And for the rest of their days on the sea,
the fearsome threesome carried out raids and

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:43.201
thefts, with no one else realizing that two
of the Caribbean’s fiercest remaining pirates

00:17:43.201 --> 00:17:48.590
were hiding breasts under their coats.

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:52.090
Rackham’s Cay

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:56.170
Unfortunately for Anne, Mary, and Calico Jack,
their adventure at sea wouldn’t last more

00:17:56.170 --> 00:17:57.170
than a few months.

00:17:57.170 --> 00:18:01.181
Leaving a relatively calm life to become a
pirate would be dangerous for anyone; leaving

00:18:01.181 --> 00:18:05.280
your pirate-snitch husband, absconding with
a former pirate, and aggressively robbing

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:10.460
every merchant ship from Mexico to the Florida
Keys – well, that’s practically suicide.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:15.180
It took all of one month for Governor Woodes
Rogers to announce a bounty for Calico Jack,

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:19.480
Anne Bonny and their crew, and another month
after that before bounty hunters were hot

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:20.480
on their trail.

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:25.480
The walls were closing around John Rackham,
and there would be no clever lie to save him

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:26.480
this time around.

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:30.810
It was only a matter of time before the pirate
hunters caught up to them, which is precisely

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:33.310
what happened in the final weeks of 1720.

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:38.140
Hunters led by Captain Jonathan Barnet boarded
the ship, meaning to bring everyone aboard

00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:40.570
back to Jamaica to be tried for piracy.

00:18:40.570 --> 00:18:44.690
Mary and Anne mounted a hellish, boisterous
defense of the pirates, but eventually, they

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:45.690
were outnumbered.

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:49.770
Once again, accounts differ here – some
say Calico Jack was too drunk below deck to

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:55.190
offer a proper defense, while other reports
say he simply recognized he was beaten and

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:57.060
quietly laid down his arms.

00:18:57.060 --> 00:19:00.770
Whatever the truth, while the women of the
ship were prepared to fight until their last

00:19:00.770 --> 00:19:03.010
breath, John Rackham offered no resistance.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:07.830
He let himself be captured and imprisoned
without much resistance, as his remaining

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:10.700
crew watched in horror and disgust.

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:13.190
Justice came swiftly for Calico Jack and his
crew.

00:19:13.190 --> 00:19:17.540
Anne Bonny and Mary Read were spared from
the gallows, as both were pregnant when captured.

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:21.070
But the remaining crew – including Rackham
– were sentenced to hang, less than a month

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:22.970
later, in Port Royal, Jamaica.

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:26.760
Before he died, Anne allegedly visited her
lover Rackham in prison.

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:30.960
He was overjoyed to see her one last time;
she merely scoffed, telling him that “if

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:35.260
you had fought like a man, you need not have
hanged like a dog.”

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:39.560
He was hanged just a few days later, on November
18, 1720, at age 37.

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:43.740
As was the case with many pirates of their
day, Rackham’s corpse was gibbeted, or hung

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:46.110
on display as a warning to other pirates.

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:50.770
The Jamaicans chose to hang Calico Jack’s
body on a small islet outside of Port Royal

00:19:50.770 --> 00:19:54.710
– a tiny piece of land known now as Rackham’s
Cay.

00:19:54.710 --> 00:19:58.930
It’s probably fitting that John Rackham’s
most lasting impact on the world around him

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:01.290
was a monument to his own failure.

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:06.350
Calico Jack was not the brightest pirate to
ever steer a schooner, nor was he the boldest.

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:11.870
In truth, he was an imperfect pirate who accomplished
very little and earned his fame primarily

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:13.270
by the company he kept.

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:17.600
He was outlived by Charles Vane, his former
mentor, who eventually found himself marooned

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:19.870
on an island in the aftermath of a hurricane.

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:24.080
He was also outlived by his partner, Anne
Bonny, who likely escaped from her Jamaican

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:25.560
prison and lived a full life.

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:30.530
The prison has no record of Bonny’s execution
or release; as a result, most histories take

00:20:30.530 --> 00:20:33.880
that to mean she slinked away and lived a
full life.

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:38.100
Her recorded death date is decades later,
in the 1780s.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:42.330
So Rackham didn’t manage to outlive most
of his peers, in the latter portion of the

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:43.620
Golden Age of Piracy.

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:47.960
He was never as well-respected, either; men
like Blackbeard and Hornigold are nearly always

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:54.060
referenced as quintessential late-era figures,
with Rackham sinking into the background.

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:57.940
Even as a pirate, he fell short – his best
prize, the Kingston, was stolen back from

00:20:57.940 --> 00:20:58.950
him after mere weeks.

00:20:58.950 --> 00:21:04.190
Perhaps worst of all, his reputation as a
pirate was blackened by too many surrenders

00:21:04.190 --> 00:21:06.190
and too few fights.

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:10.680
He went to his death disrespected by his former
crew and confidants.

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:13.750
Captain Jack was indeed one of the worst pirates
you’ve ever heard of.

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:23.810
But if he was here to defend himself today,
he might just point out that you have, in

00:21:23.810 --> 00:21:36.360
fact, heard of him.

